\details{
\strong{What comes from where.} The model's parameter types, transforms,
defaults and data-generating function are those of the \emph{analytic twin}, the
EMC2 model the network approximates (the card's \code{model} field: \code{"DDM"},
\code{"RDM"}, \code{"LNR"} or \code{"LBA"}, or \code{twin}). An un-sampled parameter therefore
takes the same default as in the analytic model (for a network without an
analytic model, see below); when that default lies
outside the network's training region (e.g. \code{sv = 0} for a DDM flow
trained on \code{sv >= 0.01}) the model refuses it, at \code{\link[=design]{design()}} time and again
when the likelihood is evaluated, instead of silently changing the model.
The likelihood, the bounds (\code{bound$minmax} = the training box) and the
refusals come from the card. Parameter vectors outside the box are rejected
(likelihood floored at \code{min_ll}) through \code{bound} and inside the evaluator.

\strong{Model card.} The fields read are \code{context_names} (the network's
parameter inputs in order), \code{context_transforms} (per name: \code{"identity"},
\code{"log"} or \code{"probit"}, the scale the network was trained on),
\code{bounds_natural} and/or \code{bounds_sampled} (the training box, \code{lower} and
\code{upper}; natural scale in the order of \code{names(context_transforms)}, sampled
scale in \code{context_names} order; if both are present they must agree), the
weights (\code{flow_mlp} + \code{classifier_mlp} + \code{spline} + \code{scaler} for
\code{"flow_joint"}, \code{mlp} + \code{spline} + \code{scaler} for \code{"flow_race"}, \code{mlp} for
\code{"regression_joint"} and \code{"mlp_joint"}), and optionally \code{model}, \code{kind}, \code{ll_floor_log},
\code{context_encoding}. A name in \code{context_transforms} that is not in
\code{context_names} was fixed when the network was trained; its value must be
given in \code{exceptions} unless the card implies it (\code{context_encoding = "st0zero"} implies \code{st0 = 0}). \code{.rds} bundles may hold an ensemble
(\code{members}, \code{"flow_joint"} only); \code{.json} cards need the \pkg{jsonlite}
package.

\strong{Kinds.}
\itemize{
\item \code{"flow_joint"}: a spline flow for rt given parameters and response plus a
classifier for the response; a two-response joint model like \link{DDM}.
\code{dfun}/\code{pfun} are the joint density and the defective CDF.
\item \code{"flow_race"}: a spline flow for one accumulator's finishing time; a race
model like \link{RDM} (\code{dfun}/\code{pfun} per accumulator; the survivor function
of the losing accumulators needs the CDF, so \code{cdf = FALSE} is refused).
\item \code{"regression_joint"}: an MLP whose single output is the joint log
density log p(rt, R | parameters) of a two-response model. Its card must
declare \code{input_layout}, the network's inputs in order, each a context
name (entered on its sampled scale) or one of \code{"rt"}, \code{"log_rt"}, \code{"R"};
optionally \code{scaler} (\code{mean}, \code{scale}; one entry per input, standardising
the whole input vector), \code{response_values} (the input values coding
responses 1 and 2; default \code{c(1, 2)}) and \code{output_scaler} (\code{mean},
\code{scale}: output = raw * scale + mean). It has no CDF. Hidden layers are
GELU (tanh approximation) or tanh; rows outside the training box have log
density \code{-Inf}.
\item \code{"mlp_joint"}: the same evaluator for a likelihood approximation network
(LAN, e.g. the published HSSM networks) converted from ONNX. The card
is written by \code{inst/scripts/onnx_to_card.py} (in the installed package:
\code{system.file("scripts", "onnx_to_card.py", package = "EMC2")}); EMC2 does
not run ONNX, a LAN being a plain MLP whose \code{.onnx} file only holds the
weights. As for \code{"regression_joint"} the card declares \code{input_layout} and
\code{response_values} (HSSM: \code{c(-1, 1)} for responses 1, 2), and in addition
\code{ll_floor_log}, the log density that rows outside the training box
return (the floor of the labels the network was trained on) instead of
\code{-Inf}. A LAN is in its own parameterisation, so give \code{p_types} (the
defaults for its parameters as it takes them) and read the mapping to
the corresponding analytic model from the card; for HSSM's
\code{ddm_uniform_st} (inputs \verb{v, a, z, t, st}) it is \code{a_DDM = 2 * a},
\code{Z = z}, \code{t0 = t - st}, \code{st0 = 2 * st}, \code{sv = SZ = 0}, \code{s = 1}.
\strong{Loading a LAN is not the same as being able to infer with it.} Its
calibration is unknown until checked: an approximation error of the
log density that varies over the parameter space biases the posterior
(for \code{ddm_uniform_st} the NLE project measured a mean score of -0.122 on
log \code{a}, where a calibrated likelihood gives 0), and the box, the floor and any
rt range the network was trained on are not enforced beyond what the card
states. Run parameter recovery and a simulation-based calibration cell
for the LAN in your own design before using it for inference.
}

\strong{Evaluation.} Networks whose \code{pre}
is \code{NULL} or a parameter name run inside EMC2's compiled likelihood (the
model's \code{c_name} is \code{"NN"}): for every particle the parameters are mapped,
passed to the network and the log-likelihood summed in C++, so fits need no
R code per particle and can use
\code{options(emc.ll_backend = "multithreaded", emc.n_threads = )}. A \code{pre}
function takes the R path (the model's \code{dfun}/\code{pfun} for each particle). Both paths give the same log-likelihood
(to rounding); both refuse censored or truncated data.

\strong{Networks without an analytic model.} Nothing requires an analytic
twin. There are two ways to supply the parameterisation yourself:
\itemize{
\item \code{p_types}: a named vector of defaults on the sampled scale, one per
network input (plus any parameter used by \code{pre} or fixed by
\code{exceptions}). Each input's transform follows the card's scale (\code{"log"}
-> \code{"exp"}, \code{"probit"} -> \code{"pnorm"}, \code{"identity"} -> \code{"identity"}); any
other parameter gets \code{"identity"} (override with \code{design(transform = )}).
The model type follows \code{kind}. There is no simulator, so \code{\link[=make_data]{make_data()}}
refuses; fit observed data, or use the next route.
\item \code{twin = function() list(type = , p_types = , transform = , rfun = )}: a
hand-written model function giving the parameterisation and, if you have
one, a simulator (\code{rfun(data, pars)} as in any EMC2 model; an optional
\code{Ttransform} is applied before it, and an optional \code{bound$minmax} bounds
parameters the network does not take as input).
}

Either way the same checks apply as with an analytic twin: a default
outside the training region is refused for an un-sampled parameter, and
every parameter must be a network input, used by \code{pre}, or fixed.

\strong{Checks that refuse a registration.} A \code{pars} that would permute the
card's inputs; a card whose \code{bounds_sampled} disagrees with its transformed
\code{bounds_natural} (e.g. permuted \code{context_names}); \code{transforms} that
disagree with the card; a network input that is not a parameter of the
twin; a twin parameter the network ignores (neither an input, nor consumed
by \code{pre}, nor fixed by \code{exceptions}); a \code{pre} that is not a shift of rt.

Loading a neural likelihood is not the same as being able to infer with it:
check calibration and identifiability (parameter recovery, likelihood
profiles, SBC) before drawing conclusions. The validation kit runs the
checks against the analytic twin: \code{\link[=nn_cell]{nn_cell()}} builds matched cells,
\code{\link[=nn_total_mass]{nn_total_mass()}}, \code{\link[=nn_score_bias]{nn_score_bias()}} and \code{\link[=nn_posterior_shift]{nn_posterior_shift()}} screen a
network in minutes, and \code{\link[=nn_sbc_cell]{nn_sbc_cell()}} runs an SBC cell with its matched
analytic control (see \code{vignette("neural-likelihoods", package = "EMC2")}).

\strong{Parallel work on macOS.} Accelerate, the BLAS EMC2 uses there,
multithreads large matrix products in a way that crashes forked processes
(parallel chains, \code{\link[=run_sbc]{run_sbc()}}); EMC2 therefore sets
\code{VECLIB_MAXIMUM_THREADS=1} when it is loaded, unless it is already set.
}
\examples{
# A shipped artefact; the card names its analytic twin (RDM)
m <- register_nn_model("rdm_small")
m()$nn$context_names

# A network with no analytic model. Here the shipped RDM flow with its
# `model` field removed stands in for your own card: give the defaults
# (sampled scale); the transforms follow the card's input scales.
card <- readRDS(system.file("extdata", "flownn", "rdm_small.rds", package = "EMC2"))
card$model <- NULL
path <- tempfile(fileext = ".rds")
saveRDS(card, path)
m2 <- register_nn_model(path, p_types = c(v = log(1), B = log(1), t0 = log(0.3),
                                          s = log(1), A = log(0.3)))
m2()$transform$func

# The same network with a simulator: a hand-written model function as twin.
# rfun(data, pars) is your simulator (here RDM's stands in for it), called
# on the parameters after Ttransform.
my_model <- function() list(
  type = "RACE",
  p_types = c(v = log(1), B = log(1), t0 = log(0.3), s = log(1), A = log(0.3)),
  transform = list(func = c(v = "exp", B = "exp", t0 = "exp", s = "exp", A = "exp")),
  Ttransform = function(pars, dadm) cbind(pars, b = pars[, "B"] + pars[, "A"]),
  rfun = RDM()$rfun)
m3 <- register_nn_model(path, twin = my_model)
}
